@page "/not-found"
@inject NavigationManager Nav

@* The app's 4xx page: what a browser gets instead of a JSON blob.

   Until this page existed, a mistyped URL answered
   `{"status":404,"path":"/does-not-exist","message":"Route not found."}` — a JSON blob on
   a blank white page with no header, no skip link, no way back — because a single
   `MapFallback` in Program.cs swallowed every unmatched path. The `<NotFound>` block in
   Routes.razor has been dead the whole time for the same reason: a browser navigation
   never reached the router.

   RENDERED BY RE-EXECUTION, NOT BY A CATCH-ALL ROUTE.
   `app.UseStatusCodePagesWithReExecute("/not-found", "?statusCode={0}")` in Program.cs
   points at this path, which is why this is a normal route rather than `/{*path}`. A
   catch-all route matches by definition, so the response says 200 — a soft 404 that tells a
   link checker, a monitor or a search engine that the page exists — and a catch-all cannot
   set the status from its own OnInitialized either, because by then Blazor has begun
   writing the body.

   BOTH of those were tried on the way here, and both shipped a 200: first the plain
   fallback (404 JSON, no page), then the catch-all (page, but 200). The re-executing status
   page is the only combination that yields a real 404 with a real body, because the
   middleware restores the ORIGINAL status after re-rendering. Do not turn this into a
   catch-all, and do not try to set `Response.StatusCode` from here.

   It also serves the other body-less 4xx responses: a 403 from a gated API endpoint is
   re-executed here too and keeps its 403, because the status is never changed by this page.
   That is why the copy branches on the status instead of assuming "404". *@

<main id="main-content" class="app-page" tabindex="-1">
    <section class="app-card not-found-card">
        <div class="app-kicker">@(StatusCode?.ToString() ?? "404")</div>
        <h1>@Heading</h1>
        <p>@Message</p>
        @if (IsMissingPage)
        {
            <ul class="not-found-links">
                <li><a href="/">Apps</a> — the catalogue</li>
                <li><a href="/azure">Azure</a> — service health, cost and uptime</li>
                <li><a href="/users">Users</a> — who has signed in</li>
                <li><a href="/settings">Settings</a> — theme, density, sound, effects</li>
            </ul>
        }
        @if (ShowPath)
        {
            <p class="app-note">Requested: <code class="not-found-path">@Path</code></p>
        }
    </section>
</main>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The status the middleware re-executed for, handed over as `?statusCode={0}`. Null when
    /// this page is reached directly, which means the same thing as 404 here.
    /// </summary>
    [SupplyParameterFromQuery(Name = "statusCode")]
    public int? StatusCode { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The requested path, echoed back so the page reads as "…and here is what you asked for"
    /// rather than as a generic error. Taken from the navigation manager rather than from the
    /// request, so it is also correct when the router's <c>&lt;NotFound&gt;</c> fragment
    /// renders this component client-side.
    ///
    /// It is the re-executed path (`/not-found`), not the original one — the middleware
    /// rewrites `Request.Path` before re-running the pipeline, and the original is not
    /// carried in the query. Echoing `/not-found` back would be worse than saying nothing, so
    /// this is only shown when the two actually differ.
    /// </summary>
    private string Path { get; set; } = "/";

    private bool IsMissingPage => StatusCode is null or 404;

    private string Heading => StatusCode switch
    {
        401 or 403 => "You are not allowed to do that",
        404 or null => "There is nothing at this address",
        _ => "That request could not be completed",
    };

    private string Message => IsMissingPage
        ? "The dashboard has exactly four pages, and none of them is this address."
        : $"This deployment refused the request. The status was {StatusCode}.";

    private bool ShowPath => !Path.Contains("not-found", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase);

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        var uri = Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri);
        Path = string.IsNullOrEmpty(uri) ? "/" : "/" + uri;
    }
}
